# Who gets asked to review what.
#
# Handles here are individual GitHub accounts, not teams. The TogetherWeOwn org
# has no teams and one member with write access, so a team would be a group of
# one. When a second person has write access, promote the functions that genuinely
# have two people to real teams; the path rules below are already split along
# those lines.
#
# This file is advisory. Earlier revisions of this comment claimed "branch
# protection enforces it" — that is not true here. The org is on the free plan
# and this repo is private, so branch protection and rulesets are unavailable and
# GitHub cannot require a code owner's approval. What this file still does is
# auto-request the owner as a reviewer when a PR opens.
#
# A rule naming an account without write access to this repo is silently dropped
# by GitHub. That is how this file broke: it named teams in the old two-gaming
# org, none of which exist here.
#
# Note: scripts/setup-github.sh and docs/GITHUB.md still describe the old
# two-gaming team model and are no longer the source of truth for this file.

# Default: everything in this repo.
*                           @Rick7C2

# Tests and CI, called out because "make CI green" is the single easiest thing to
# get wrong on purpose.
/test/                      @Rick7C2
/.github/workflows/         @Rick7C2

# The contract the website reads. A change here can break two-web silently.
/docs/EVENTS.md             @Rick7C2

# Secrets, privacy and deploy posture. Changes here are reviewed, always.
/docs/SECRETS.md            @Rick7C2
/docs/PRIVACY.md            @Rick7C2
/deploy/                    @Rick7C2
/.gitignore                 @Rick7C2
/.env.example               @Rick7C2
